By default, newly created threads are non-daemon. 
The Java Virtual Machine terminates when all non-daemon threads have finished, even if daemon threads are still running.
A background thread is a thread that performs supporting work behind the main application tasks. 
A daemon thread is commonly used for background work because it does not prevent the JVM from terminating.
Daemon threads are useful for background or support tasks, such as
Garbage collection.
Background monitoring.
Cleanup tasks.
Supporting other application threads.
The Java Virtual Machine does not wait for daemon threads to finish.
If all non-daemon threads finish, the Java Virtual Machine can exit even if daemon threads are still running.
